A||||12||87ECO5||Compiled notes on interior restoration/improvements||||1||
Z||000000||87ECO5||04-03-2000||01:30 PM||||Getting ready to begin a restoration/update project on my coupe.  Wanted to summarize what I've read on this forum and make sure it's still correct.  Actually, I think this could be a good reference if I could employ help from some of you to make this document thoroughly complete.<P><B>Carpeting</B> - There are several sources for replacement carpeting - The Fiero Store and J.C. Witney are two.  However, the general consensus is that the carpeting from Mr. Mike's is the best.  For about $200, you get two floor pieces, two door pieces, one firewall piece and two floor mats.  Most (including Mr. Mike's) suggest placing the new carpet over the existing carpet.  To install, every panel that touches the carpeting must first be removed.<P><B>Gauges</B> - A few options exist to update/improve the look and function of the Fiero gauges.  First, there are the many different digital packages.  The one I'm most familiar with (which isn't saying much) is the one offered by our own Cliff Pennock.  For those with the backlit gauges, SuperXMotorsports offers a set of replacement faces.  They are available in several different face and lighting color configurations and are fairly easy to install.  Those with the reflector style gauges have the most options.  You can either upgrade to a digital package, upgrade to a backlit style then purchase the SXM faces or keep the stock appearance, or you can purchase a set of decals (I found mine on eBay) that cover the existing faces.<P><B>Headliner</B> - One option is to recover it yourself.  Doing so gives you many more color and fabric options.  The biggest caution is that the panel becomes brittle over time and can easily break if not handled carefully.  Another option is Mr. Mike's.  He offers a few color and fabric choices (felt and perforated vinyl).  Unfortunately, the perforated vinyl isn't available for cars with sunroofs.  The Fiero Store also sells replacement headliners.  I've not heard any comments on these.<P><B>Matrix Panels</B> - There are quite a few options here.  Several places (The Fiero Store and Mr. Mike's to name two) offer dozens of options for dressing up or covering up the panels that surround our gauges, shifters and HVAC controls.  Varying greatly in price from less than $100 to more than $400, these panel covers come in an array of different hardwood and carbon fiber finishes.  Yet another option (cheaper and - some say - with results that match the more expensive coverings) is the use of a $5 roll of shelf paper purchased from a local department store and covered with a $5 can of automotive lacquer.  When done properly, many people say you can have a professional look without having to mortgage the house.<P><B>Plastic and Vinyl Panels</B> - There are basically two options in improving or changing the look of the plastic and vinyl panels - either replace them with new(er) parts or paint them with a paint made specifically for plastic and vinyl.  The first option is pretty much self-explanatory.  It's also easier but you only have a handful of color choices (a few grays and a few tans) and it can get expensive.  The second option is a little trickier but allows more options.  The paint, made by Plasti-Kote and available at any auto parts outlet, comes in an aerosol can and offers about a dozen different colors.  To use, simply remove the panel from the car, clean with a mild detergent, mask off what you don't want sprayed, and apply two or three LIGHT coats (heavy coats will cover the texture of the panel).  NOTE:  If you've used a dressing on the panel, you must wait at least one month before painting.  This paint is guaranteed to not bubble or peel and the stories I've heard say it's very durable.<P><B>Seats</B> - Again, the possibilities are endless.  Prices and options extend from the $35 cloth covers at Wal-mart to the more expensive $400 leather covers from Mr. Mike's.  Rumor has it that Mr. Mike's is now developing a cloth version of his cover also.  Yet another option is the installation of factory seats from the F-body (prices can/will vary quite a bit depending on condition and the mood of the junkyard owner that day!).  The conversion is simple.  Either re-drill the F-body rails to fit onto the Fiero studs or fasten the Fiero rails to the seat in place of the F-body rails.<P>So, is the data contained within this document still current?  Have I missed or incorrectly stated anything important?<P>Now, I have a question regarding my project.  I am planning to completely change the color of my interior.  After I replace the carpeting, seat covers and headliner, recover the speaker grills and repaint the plastic and vinyl panels, everything will be the new color … everything except the sun visors.  What options do I have to make these match the new theme?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BENGAL4||04-03-2000||03:04 PM||||Scott,<P>Do not, I repeat DO NOT, buy your carpet from the Fiero Store. I wish I would have bought mine from Mr. Mikes. The Fiero Stores, is terrible quality and in certain light, my grey looks green. I am going to get Mr. mikes, this summer. As for seats, go with the leather. It looks and smells great. ||||reg||13
Z||000002||Racer 1||04-03-2000||03:49 PM||||Leather seats arent fun in the 90+ degree sun!||||reg||8
Z||000003||Old Lar||04-03-2000||08:34 PM||||I think Mr. Mike is offering recovered sun visors.  Give him the old ones and get replacement new ones.||||reg||3
Z||000004||2M4 Dale||04-04-2000||12:05 AM||||I'm about to start on mine.(took the headliner out Saturday) I hope I can get it back to stock. All the aftermarket stuff is nice but I like the stock look. I wouldn't mind changing the color to all charcoal gray though.||||reg||6
Z||000005||Dan86gt3.8||04-04-2000||03:16 AM||||What can you do if the vinal is becoming detached from around the radio and ac controlls?  I got a replacement piece from a junk yard but didn't realize that a regular one won't work with a gt pod gauges above the radio.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||04-04-2000||09:29 AM||||Gorilla Glue and wood to hold it in place.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||04-04-2000||11:46 PM||||Wood,and duct tape,or anything else that'll help hold it overnight.<BR>only patch a small area at a time also,will work better that way,even though it's tempting to put glue on everything at once.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||04-06-2000||10:26 PM||||When my wife and I recovered the headliner we also recovered the sunvisors.  It's pretty simple.  She used a regular Sears sewing machine to very slowly sew the fabric around the edges.  Other than not having the original map pockets they look like OEM.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87ECO5||04-12-2000||08:37 AM||||Man, I completely forgot about the seat belts.  Once the interior color is changed, and I've successfully recovered the sun visors, I'll still have gray seat belts.<P>Is there a way to change the color of the seat belts?  Can the belts from other GM cars be used instead?<P>Any help is greatly appreciated.  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||04-12-2000||09:32 AM||||Our seatbelts are a single loop.  I think most of the others actually have ends on them.  (You know what I mean.)  I've heard that you can dye the belts but it might weaken them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||04-12-2000||02:32 PM||||From what I have read, there's not much you can do to safely change the color of the seat belt.  I've heard the the interior color revolves around the color of the seat belts...||||reg||1
Z||000013||lennyb4||04-12-2000||03:13 PM||||Hey, i know there are places where you can get different colored belts. You might want to try places like Summit Auto, H.R.P., or i'm sure someone here, maybe fierodave, monkeyman (or should i say MONKEYKING...lol) or the ogre might know other places.||||reg||1
